      "writtenOn": "2026-11-19T21:28:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Passing `not-ipair?` as the list comparator\u0027s `empty?` makes every non-ipair \"empty\", so all non-ipairs are equal and sort before ipairs. With `c \u003d (make-improper-ilist-comparator (make-default-comparator))`:\n\n- `(\u003d? c 1 2)` is #t.\n- `(\u003d? c (ipair 1 2) (ipair 1 3))` is #t, because the tails 2 and 3 compare equal.\n- `(\u003c? c (ipair 1 \u0027()) 5)` is #f, and `(\u003c? c 5 (ipair 1 \u0027()))` is #t.\n- 1 and 2 hash alike.\n\nThe spec: \"the empty list precedes all ipairs, which precede all other objects. Ipairs are compared as if with (make-ipair-comparator comparator comparator). All other objects are compared using comparator.\" The regression test (368-370) only checks equal ipairs, which pass by accident. Write it directly with three ranks (() \u003c ipair \u003c other), recursing for ipairs and using `comparator` for the rest, with a hash to match.",
      "revId": "67466ad6527d84b5b1e1c52b96cb79bb4cc0586d",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
