      "writtenOn": "2026-11-19T21:28:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`ipair-comparator` and `ilist-comparator` compare elements with SRFI 128\u0027s default comparator, which knows nothing of ipairs: it compares them with `eqv?` and has no order for them.\n\n- `(\u003d? ilist-comparator (iq (1)) (iq (1)))` is #f, while `equal?` says #t.\n- `(\u003c? ilist-comparator (iq (1)) (iq (2)))` raises \"no ordering for this type of object\".\n\nSince `iq` makes every nested list an ilist, any ilist of ilists breaks, e.g. as a (srfi 146) key. The header (20) admits it, but the spec defines these over the default comparator. Use a library-local element comparator that recurses into ipairs (and hashes them), so that what other libraries get from `default-hash` doesn\u0027t change. On 1260924 I\u0027ve asked that standard libraries not register with `comparator-register-default!`.",
      "revId": "67466ad6527d84b5b1e1c52b96cb79bb4cc0586d",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
